{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479316} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575547}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant Squared"} :step/id #uuid "1eb256c9-e635-52b0-9693-979de7534909" :step/math-academy-id 1387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064055 :question/id #uuid "c2c57145-3988-5ce8-b13f-114bba6964f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9836abbe-e172-5c2f-8b19-145ef6d5c02d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479237} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575586}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"} :step/id #uuid "ffd7ef8c-69c3-5ccb-ac1c-da42a0605f1c" :step/math-academy-id 1389}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064392}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425} :activity/id #uuid "0693eaa1-2cbe-58a3-aa5d-c539aa74793e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/id #uuid "c2580f8e-3ba3-5aa9-b1f1-0d8e49986381" :topic/math-academy-id 1109} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" :tutorial/id #uuid "958f67f2-8e00-5818-8af7-9f88561ef84c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef3171cb-fb23-5d4d-8819-73da9533de18" :step/math-academy-id 12327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530651} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} :step/id #uuid "e27ef05d-bc90-5cce-9885-8f21279044e8" :step/math-academy-id 12328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571450}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "6cbd3b19-1c1f-519e-b232-315484ae9234" :step/math-academy-id 12329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c6d5393-f91c-57e5-999c-92eedf550f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2623 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "4a3a2bef-3f64-510c-8bf8-25d81169d680" :step/math-academy-id 12330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536099} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "138782c6-ad9e-5b08-b3dd-375476961e1e" :step/math-academy-id 12331}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096889}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430} :activity/id #uuid "a699a755-f56a-5a6a-8493-60b83f42eaa5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/id #uuid "c54eaff0-4e6f-57d2-aa08-401a88379d23" :topic/math-academy-id 1110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dd2362a7-73d2-577f-aa31-2150f8f6b63f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ed5427f5-4392-5166-a825-dd9dcb07cc3d" :step/math-academy-id 12332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc978820-517c-5885-b31b-ab6f9650d57a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492115} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572721}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "6859d258-9c69-5bab-88e5-4b946166e00d" :step/math-academy-id 12333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488448} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572683}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f384d7ca-2c43-5aae-b786-23e8a903bb4f" :step/math-academy-id 12334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ed29747-9007-50b8-9347-89e46eabc182" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2625 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "185d55a4-49d5-53d5-95b3-8eb93729ad7f" :step/math-academy-id 12335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572670}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "f7097f26-3f43-5e1d-8496-783599f1b7ac" :step/math-academy-id 12336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "584707ea-2c96-5389-926d-b9ac87adc17c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2626 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "c001eb53-21b0-5423-a50f-1e506d151f8c" :step/math-academy-id 12337}] :activity/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096890}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327} :activity/id #uuid "bfd96d16-131a-5887-9f3c-822479cc8e6b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/math-academy-id 987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493326} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547057}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "978ac109-c615-5055-9e0a-f164ca3a1990" :step/math-academy-id 22383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093442 :tutorial/content "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{1cm} m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}.\n$$\n\nHere, $m=-\\dfrac{1}{f'(x_1)}$ because the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line, and $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $A(1,1).$ This normal line is the dashed line shown below, which is perpendicular to the solid tangent line. \n\n![](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nAt $A(1,1),$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nf'(1) \t= 2(1) = 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, we want the normal line, and the slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm = -\\dfrac{1}{f'(1)} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we work out the equation of the normal line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is $y=-\\dfrac x 2 + \\dfrac 3 2.$ We could also write this in standard form as $x+2y=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c999b-ac30-5116-acbd-795f13cc2b3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 359 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a4d7867-7ef2-52ad-93ae-c3ea0c556954" :step/math-academy-id 667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480110} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524021}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "ee29e21c-ec79-5cef-8080-e39f4ded4176" :step/math-academy-id 668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493324} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523994}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"} :step/id #uuid "434b8ac4-0cfe-5365-987b-8d6de7b04e04" :step/math-academy-id 671}] :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092461}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547100} :activity/id #uuid "fad01a8c-be82-5d4b-b516-bfef7fe94dc5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/math-academy-id 987} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142b6536-fed4-529f-ac1d-8a42fc666730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244498} {:answer/id #uuid "65112d5b-e5af-58b2-8b42-2fafe84f3bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245208} {:answer/id #uuid "6e29c4b1-5a97-5a97-bbf2-51eabed39e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245289} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93f7a3-eace-5053-ae98-706828a1b4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246111} {:answer/id #uuid "d23fbfd4-1cbd-54d1-aa24-886860426aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246184} :answer-field/id #uuid "816b14ca-c9ce-5b90-8b9c-06306f5bacc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24c7ef87-44ff-5aaa-8c80-717e24087d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-11124" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+2 \\\\ y & =-x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0+3 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,3).$"} :step/id #uuid "273eb5b8-c26e-526c-b817-5fd744a9c5cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3826d54-f39e-5e45-982c-0196c96b30c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090533} {:answer/id #uuid "bf822091-623a-5b75-a700-06562c3fa7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090534} {:answer/id #uuid "c014a862-aa95-5ee5-8cc4-90a1115cf633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090535} {:answer/id #uuid "e535f72e-5b43-5878-a978-9a20e1d8470d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090536} {:answer/id #uuid "48a27df3-0a04-52a4-9003-fd2c61c07e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186090534} :answer-field/id #uuid "7907bce1-6e91-548c-bf8c-f50341390d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a649b33-83ae-5424-9852-7d09d082fffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47406" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,8)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-64 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8y & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=8+66=74.$"} :step/id #uuid "6d424a59-d0d5-5dc4-afe5-f773d34fca45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546771} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa428138-3fdd-52e3-90d7-e57b79a7836a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546543} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fb6fa9-d25d-5930-b90b-ff3334996e24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870" :question/math-academy-id "q-342187" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-\\frac{2}{x}$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{2}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4+2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=4+2=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{6}[x-(-1)] \\\\ -6(y+2) & =x+1 \\\\ -6y-12 & =x+1 \\\\ x+6\\cdot y & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dfa4a8d0-084b-5540-8862-0031ccbeff76"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546252}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116} :activity/id #uuid "9ed557a5-e06f-5f5a-9230-32a93a80b727" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/id #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" :topic/math-academy-id 1007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093369 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate exponential functions like \n\n$$\ny = e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=3x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf67b8fb-2658-5221-b309-6cf09e93b5e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1268 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "87b44f73-3af6-53c8-a8c8-61525491b5d4" :step/math-academy-id 646582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546080} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546072}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "a179fdba-d910-50b0-81ea-369c06993adf" :step/math-academy-id 646583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562394}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "a8a87a08-8a33-5f7d-a2ee-3bb4f46035fe" :step/math-academy-id 646589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534177} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562416}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"} :step/id #uuid "e3bd15e6-53e0-51f6-bb04-9b4ea73b76c4" :step/math-academy-id 646586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093118}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092399}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562581} :activity/id #uuid "d99ecb5e-abe9-5c9f-a181-aa603fce82c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/id #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" :topic/math-academy-id 1007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265b541b-62a2-540d-bd79-5ef733742b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285123} {:answer/id #uuid "4a81ce5d-011d-51ee-bf9c-6333bf4dc66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285478} {:answer/id #uuid "4b97230b-e5a8-5328-9497-45595399fb66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285492} {:answer/id #uuid "812af0ad-7d34-5c50-a85a-78aa2431dcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd5724-68fc-54e4-9b62-8ebbbd324624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfdfe24-c411-57d2-aa42-0fbbe96ad9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e52fa16-cf3e-5339-95e2-786fe7ef7e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1953" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01785848-3535-566c-8ed3-1625e925070f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbe0539-4fe2-5131-9a91-4a158b45aa23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{{x}^{4}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561701} {:answer/id #uuid "699ef952-cd7a-5cde-b182-3f4d9a8b1d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561845} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4af48c-3147-5fd2-ba59-b1551123c8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x{e}^{2x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561887} {:answer/id #uuid "ca809957-cb8d-5918-bf34-5e74f50b4780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}{e}^{2{x}^{4}+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562088} {:answer/id #uuid "f16911d4-15e8-5cbf-a535-476b2410db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x{e}^{6{x}^{4}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561845} :answer-field/id #uuid "018265d8-4b38-5450-a523-ac3f8812815c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f55f57b-ca3e-54c4-9349-d3c4559745cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71877" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{2{x}^{4}+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{4}+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "734de382-5fea-5f07-968d-7e70fd7866b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38229b23-2b39-59e5-9abd-c6c07d94e182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\cdot {4}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561722} {:answer/id #uuid "78c9ae9f-b6bd-5e2b-a491-13f9286f6a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561883} {:answer/id #uuid "799b33bd-f993-5922-93a7-8e0d15933029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561884} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccc347a-a561-5923-a18e-8613311aa3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561891} {:answer/id #uuid "b13acdb7-c3c6-501a-bb40-9e3bfb33ea7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562024} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0be4a0-ce70-5992-8eed-814e9a761b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0876dfcc-e11e-5c1c-a9e0-0c7b6f68915a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1951" :question/problem "If $f(x)={4}^{7x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d84d7d23-dca4-52c0-a782-75952a8d43b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562521}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561576}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528} :activity/id #uuid "9be3f59b-09db-52f4-97b2-cfb6afd98870" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/id #uuid "476d32c8-5b16-54ef-9688-daca6c7937ad" :topic/math-academy-id 280} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060893 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "dca4e1b4-5f89-5c88-9f24-b4671410d644" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "970c03a9-20ec-54a6-81c1-0b04852bff2d" :step/math-academy-id 12320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489497} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570758}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "727b4e4a-8b24-5865-8b3f-cc8d94213ab1" :step/math-academy-id 12321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483539} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570762}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"} :step/id #uuid "3ba7adb6-6e48-5f3e-af46-a386f761686f" :step/math-academy-id 12322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a25dc2a-846b-5554-9cc7-682b81f17e0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2619 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Sine"} :step/id #uuid "58bef47e-2b3d-546b-87f7-1097147174c7" :step/math-academy-id 12324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." :tutorial/id #uuid "40bcba62-6d88-5cfd-9d89-e773514e5a63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2620 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "eb4d0827-6392-5186-8463-5ccc6e4e3392" :step/math-academy-id 12325 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060896 :tutorial/content "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" :tutorial/id #uuid "32a1aa14-0e0a-53c1-a40c-e728e4b231c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2621 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "2b5a3334-fa23-5bc8-bf82-7d80f190e65d" :step/math-academy-id 12326}] :activity/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059951}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534} :activity/id #uuid "10bdaba2-b2d6-5479-a66a-9f5c1d933300" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/id #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" :topic/math-academy-id 281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060897 :tutorial/content "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{′} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =({f}^{′}(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{′} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "947c462e-76d9-5248-93f8-a558f1ad16c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9931d327-3dfa-546d-9bad-52bfcf096bda" :step/math-academy-id 12357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "67b86b8b-be5c-54ba-b03c-e422581c1992" :step/math-academy-id 12358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060898 :tutorial/content "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f21f7901-ec61-5c3d-a6f2-6bba03a55a17" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2634 :tutorial/title "Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8fa38988-5cde-5291-bf68-d15bba187f2a" :step/math-academy-id 12359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484417} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570784}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "65dcc063-d790-5cae-ba88-b2b94d516359" :step/math-academy-id 12360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060899 :tutorial/content "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bfdba2-25dc-51af-97f8-00b3b216d0a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2635 :tutorial/title "Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "2c85fed7-69ed-56ea-8cfa-78cf775d9d35" :step/math-academy-id 12361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570716}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "75f9df48-9a05-5828-82cb-52a03412297c" :step/math-academy-id 12362}] :activity/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059952}]}
